@@s001 Trap pairs
@@s002 Trap pairs
@@s003 Two problems with the same input and one different word: how one detail changes the paradigm, with the real pairs from the exams.
@@s004 The principle
@@s005 The teacher builds the exams in pairs: same input, one different word. Learn to see the <b>word</b>, not the topic. When two problems in the same exam look alike, one is almost always the easy version (greedy or traversal) and the other the hard one (DP or NP-hard). Ask yourself: does this word break the exchange argument? does it remove the order? does it force choosing a subset of nodes?
@@s006 The pairs
@@s007 version A
@@s008 paradigm A
@@s009 version B (the hard one)
@@s010 Mink Farms: close more farms with ≤ k mink
@@s011 greedy (smallest first)
@@s012 Mink Massacre: kill more mink with ≤ k → DP subset sum O(nk)
@@s013 Pioneer: more plots with 1000 credits
@@s014 greedy
@@s015 Area: maximum area with C credits → DP knapsack O(nC)
@@s016 Books: more books, distinct authors
@@s017 greedy (the shortest per author)
@@s018 Completionist: more pages → DP subset sum O(nm)
@@s019 Strawberries: k slices, more strawberries
@@s020 greedy top-k
@@s021 Budget cake: budget b → DP knapsack O(nb)
@@s022 Interval scheduling, all jobs worth 1
@@s023 greedy (earliest finish)
@@s024 Weighted interval scheduling (different weights) → DP OPT(j)
@@s025 Coins: the system is canonical
@@s026 greedy (cashier)
@@s027 Any coins, exact change → DP O(Tn)
@@s028 Don't be a square / Avoid: a path that avoids nodes
@@s029 BFS after removing them
@@s030 All squares / More: a path that collects more → NP-hard
@@s031 Forced / Be a square: disjoint paths to the special ones
@@s032 flow (disjoint paths)
@@s033 More: how many more red nodes → NP-hard (one vs many)
@@s034 MoreDAG: path in a DAG
@@s035 DP on DAG
@@s036 More: the same in an undirected graph → NP-hard (acyclicity gives an order)
@@s037 Some cycle: find a cycle
@@s038 DFS (back edge)
@@s039 Big cycle: cycle of length ≥ r → NP-hard
@@s040 Diameter: maximum shortest paths
@@s041 BFS from every node
@@s042 longest simple path → NP-hard (in a DAG: DP)
@@s043 Pipeline / Isolation: min cut
@@s044 flow (min cut)
@@s045 max cut → NP-hard
@@s046 Vertex cover in a tree or bipartite graph
@@s047 DP or flow (Kőnig)
@@s048 Vertex cover in any graph → NP-hard
@@s049 Pairing between two sets
@@s050 flow
@@s051 3D matching with triples → NP-hard
@@s052 Minimum-cost spanning tree
@@s053 greedy (Kruskal, Prim)
@@s054 Spanning tree with exactly k leaves → NP-hard (k = 2 is Hamiltonian path)
@@s055 2SAT
@@s056 3SAT (Vegan) → NP-hard
@@s057 Line (domino): counts only the final value
@@s058 traversal on the components
@@s059 longest possible line → much harder
@@s060 Stacks: two exact halves
@@s061 NP-hard (Partition)
@@s062 Mette: minimum number of parties (greedy)
@@s063 Practical rule
@@s064 When two problems in the same exam look alike, the word that separates them is the detail that counts. Do not stop at the topic: re-read the verb and the constraint.
@@s065 Mistakes that cost points
@@s066 <b>«Sort the input»</b> without saying for what and in which direction. The text forbids it explicitly.
@@s067 <b>Time without parameters</b>: writing O(n) when the problem has N and K. Declare what the symbols mean.
@@s068 <b>Reinventing</b>: describing the BFS line by line instead of citing it. It is enough to say "BFS" and its cost.
@@s069 <b>Reduction the wrong way round</b>: starting from the new problem as if it were given. Start from the known problem, as in the table of <i>Easy look-alikes</i>. In question (d) it gets 0 points.
@@s070 <b>OPT without meaning</b>: a recurrence without saying what <code>OPT(i, j)</code> represents and the range of the indices.
@@s071 <b>Counterexample with ties</b>: if the algorithm could choose well by breaking a tie, the counterexample is not «guaranteed to fail». The counterexample must hold for every choice.
